WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.832
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:08.720
DAVID ARONCHICK: Hi, everyone.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.090
Thanks so much for coming.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:13.030
I'm David Aronchick,
PM here at Google,

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:14.980
working on a project
called Kubeflow.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:16.510
And we're here to
talk about how you

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:20.860
bring machine learning
to multi-cloud hybrid

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:21.790
environments.

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:25.072
Particularly as an entire
stack where it's not just

00:00:25.072 --> 00:00:26.530
thinking about the
machine learning

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:30.820
workload but the data and all
the other components as well.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.980
I always like to start
in this very basic way.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:36.010
Everyone has a different idea
about what machine learning is.

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:39.340
In this case, machine learning
starts with a question.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:40.960
It always starts
with a question.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.510
And so you might take
a sample question like,

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:44.980
how much is my house worth?

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:46.720
And then you come
up with some ideas

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.004
about how you might think
about how you would structure

00:00:50.004 --> 00:00:51.170
the answer to that question.

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:53.753
Well, maybe it's based on square
footage, number of bathrooms,

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:54.860
location, whatever.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:58.580
But on one axis you
choose that question

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:00.360
and on the second
axis that's where you

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:01.480
should choose your house price.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.563
In this case, you go out
and collect a data point.

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:06.820
Well, if it's this big it's
going to cost this much money.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:08.710
And then you collect
some more data points

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:11.890
and then you collect some more
data points and eventually you

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:12.750
get a line.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.309
You draw this line and then
you answer your question

00:01:15.309 --> 00:01:17.350
using all that data on
the line you've collected.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:21.580
You say, OK, I have 2,100
square feet, my house is worth,

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:23.780
I don't know, $339,000.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.262
Not in San Francisco of
course, but you get the idea.

00:01:27.262 --> 00:01:29.470
So congratulations, you're
a machine learning expert.

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:33.650
With that, you can leave, we'll
have certificates at the door.

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:36.575
But of course, things
get complicated.

00:01:36.575 --> 00:01:38.200
And in this case,
you could have things

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.330
like non-linear groupings
where you have things

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:42.100
by neighborhood or
things like that.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:46.330
Maybe it's multi-dimensional,
maybe things change over time.

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:49.030
The reality is that that is the
essence of machine learning.

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:52.216
Where that first thing is
clean, linear regression.

00:01:52.216 --> 00:01:54.340
Do you want to sound very
smart among your friends?

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.270
It's not a line, it's
a linear regression.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:00.850
Machine learning is a way
to answer those questions

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.820
that you start with
without explicitly knowing

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.110
how to get there.

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:07.270
You collect a whole
bunch of features

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:10.360
and then you ask the system to
come up with an answer for you.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.670
At Google we've
done this as well

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:16.090
and we had great
success around this.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:18.880
Google obviously hires some
of the best data center

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:20.280
engineers in the world.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.810
We're very, very proud
of how efficient we are.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:25.060
We measure everything in
what's called Power Usage

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:25.870
Effectiveness.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.390
So these are people who know
everything there is to know.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:29.973
They're using renewable
power, they're

00:02:29.973 --> 00:02:31.750
using the latest technology.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:34.630
And what they want to do is they
want to be the most efficient

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.330
around this electricity, to
stick all this electricity in

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.154
and get the most
amount of compute out.

00:02:39.154 --> 00:02:40.570
And they have a
lot of ideas about

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:42.130
what makes a difference
here but what

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:43.946
they decided is, why
don't we associate

00:02:43.946 --> 00:02:45.070
this with machine learning?

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:47.560
We'll put all the features,
all the data we collect,

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.270
into the system and see
how we can improve things.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:52.990
So what they did was
they graphed everything,

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:56.149
they turned machine learning
on and the graph came down,

00:02:56.149 --> 00:02:57.940
and then they turn the
machine learning off

00:02:57.940 --> 00:02:58.900
and it came back up.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:00.400
And again, these
are the best people

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.800
in the world who thought
they knew exactly how to make

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.450
the system run
great and they were

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.670
able to get massive
improvement by associating

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:08.230
with machine learning.

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:09.880
We say this and
we as an industry

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:11.440
do a terrible job around this.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:13.606
Because we see this-- we
show off these great signs,

00:03:13.606 --> 00:03:16.630
oh it can solve cancer,
detect heart disease,

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:19.780
massively improve efficiency--
and people say it's great.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:22.300
Except it's also
very, very hard.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.490
And it's not just hard
because I'm bad at math.

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:27.400
It is hard because
there's this massive gap.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:30.194
Most folks are collecting
data and they don't really

00:03:30.194 --> 00:03:32.110
know what to do with it
or how to structure it

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:33.500
and anything like that.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:36.550
And then they want to move over
and get to that ML goodness

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:38.410
but there's lots of
pain in the middle

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:41.345
and let me tell you about what
some of that pain looks like.

00:03:41.345 --> 00:03:43.720
The reason is is because it
basically comes down to this.

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.000
You really today
only have two options

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.880
when it comes to rolling out
a machine learning pipeline.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:49.920
Excuse me.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.660
You have do it yourself,
which is great,

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:54.825
except you have to
set up from scratch,

00:03:54.825 --> 00:03:56.950
you have to integrate with
all those legacy systems

00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:58.810
and make sure things
are working properly,

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:02.050
and eventually, when it comes to
migrate based on your business

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:02.570
needs--

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:05.110
and there's always a
reason to migrate--

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:07.180
you have to go and you
have to do that all again.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:08.530
That's a huge pain.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:11.320
It does work but it
takes a lot of work.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:13.450
Or you could go hosted.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:16.339
Hosted it is fine in
those first five minutes

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:19.180
but then you have
the next five years.

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:20.649
You have all of
the things that you

00:04:20.649 --> 00:04:23.020
had to deal with on
the left, integrating,

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:27.490
dealing with legacy, plus
hosted is single cloud only.

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:30.010
And the reality is is
folks are not single cloud

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:31.750
and I'll get to
them in a second.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:33.790
To be clear that's not
all hosted solutions

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:37.470
but it's certainly the
most common situation.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.610
Haven't we heard
this story before?

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:41.130
And the reality is we have.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:43.680
This was the story when
we first introduced

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:45.240
Containers and Kubernetes.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.310
The idea was that everyone
was out there rolling

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:50.100
their own bespoke solutions
for rolling out workloads,

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.930
for doing service
discovery, having

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:55.350
things componentize
and work together,

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:57.357
but that was not efficient.

00:04:57.357 --> 00:04:59.190
Things wouldn't work
as you moved from cloud

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:01.649
to cloud, on-prem to cloud.

00:05:01.649 --> 00:05:03.690
Things wouldn't even work
within different zones.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:05.106
Containers and
Kubernetes gave you

00:05:05.106 --> 00:05:08.790
an explicit, declarative way
to roll out your applications

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:10.740
in a way that made sense.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:15.030
In Austin at 2017 we
talked about Kubernetes

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:18.270
being not just a platform
for rolling it out but being

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:19.740
really extensible.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:23.100
Letting you take Kubernetes and
extend it in a way that makes

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:24.180
sense to you.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:26.970
And that was the essence of
what we called Cloud Native

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:27.750
Computing.

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:29.370
Using that platform
of Kubernetes

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:33.630
to extend it in a
very elegant way.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:35.310
As a result, you
saw this huge amount

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:39.390
of uptake of what's
called Cloud Native Apps.

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:43.110
These declarative, highly
microservice oriented,

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:44.650
highly distributed systems.

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:49.480
So what we need is
Cloud Native ML.

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:51.620
Cloud Native ML looks
like the following.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:53.235
It is composable--
it lets you piece

00:05:53.235 --> 00:05:54.610
a number of pieces
together based

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:55.870
on your business requirements.

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:58.540
It's highly portable-- it
lets you move from place

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:00.370
to place very readily.

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:02.860
And it's highly scalable-- it
takes advantage of the fact

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:06.290
that you now have effectively
unlimited compute.

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:08.270
By this we mean the following.

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:10.100
First, composability.

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:11.610
Most folks when
they think about ML,

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:13.984
they think about starting
with just building a model

00:06:13.984 --> 00:06:14.650
and that's true.

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:16.730
Building a model is
obviously a core component.

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:19.250
But it is just one component.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:23.252
A standard ML pipe might look
much more like this, where

00:06:23.252 --> 00:06:25.460
you're spending a bunch of
time doing data ingestion,

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:28.804
analysis transformation, then
finally you get to your model

00:06:28.804 --> 00:06:30.470
and you want to do
distributed training.

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:32.930
And finally you have to
roll it out production.

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:36.170
An ML system needs to
support all of these things

00:06:36.170 --> 00:06:37.940
in order to be production ready.

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:41.660
Second, you want it to be
portable and portable looks

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:42.540
like the following.

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:46.880
First, when you think about
your overall stack of compute,

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:48.280
it's obviously not just compute.

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:49.880
CPU cycles are just one thing.

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:52.430
You might have any of the
following associated with it

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:54.840
where that entire pipeline
that I described before,

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:57.260
is just a portion of that.

00:06:57.260 --> 00:06:59.750
But Multi-Cloud is the reality.

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:02.780
You could have this great stack
that looks awesome in one place

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:07.370
but 81% of you are likely to
have multiple of these stacks.

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:09.140
And not just one multiple stack.

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:12.290
In fact, the average
person has up to five.

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:15.380
What that looks like is, where
you have this experimentation

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:18.450
workload running, potentially
on-prem or on your laptop,

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:20.159
you might have a
completely different one

00:07:20.159 --> 00:07:22.449
when you get to training and
a completely different one

00:07:22.449 --> 00:07:23.520
when you get to cloud.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:26.120
And that now just multiplies
your overall matrices

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:28.560
times three.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:30.950
I know what you're saying,
this might not matter to me.

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:35.570
You're wrong and I can
prove it in one exercise.

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:38.920
Raise your hand if
you have a laptop.

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:39.880
Congratulations!

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:42.040
You're all multi-cloud.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:43.930
And don't just trust
me, listen to this guy.

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:45.760
He invented Kubernetes.

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:47.710
"The way I think about
it: every difference

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:50.620
between dev/staging/prod
will eventually

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:51.650
result in an outage."

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:52.650
Did you catch that word?

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:53.990
Let me underline for you.

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:55.030
Got that?

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:55.893
Dev.

00:07:55.893 --> 00:07:57.520
Dev!

00:07:57.520 --> 00:07:58.710
Dev is an environment.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:07:59.970
Dev is a cloud.

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:01.510
Dev is an environment
that you have

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:04.450
to replicate over and over
and over again and move.

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:06.840
Your laptop counts.

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:09.570
And this column right
here tends to be the one.

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:12.600
That is your experimentation
and it is something

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:15.740
that when your data scientists
are working on their experiment

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:17.490
and they go to their
production scientists

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:20.100
and they say, all right, it's
time for you to run this model.

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:23.100
They're like, I can't use any
of this stuff that you ran,

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:24.940
and that's not good.

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:26.982
And then finally, when we
think about scalability

00:08:26.982 --> 00:08:28.440
we think about all
of these things.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:32.340
Now, many people just say, OK,
it's just GPUs, TPUs, CPUs.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:35.549
But really scalability is human
beings and it's skill sets

00:08:35.549 --> 00:08:37.409
and it's number of experiments.

00:08:37.409 --> 00:08:40.110
You need a system that can
handle all of these things.

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:41.490
So, Cloud Native ML--

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:43.919
composable, portable,
and scalable.

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:45.870
You know what's really
good at these things?

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:48.170
Containers and Kubernetes.

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:52.820
Except, of course, if you want
to use ML on Kubernetes today

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:54.780
you have to become
an expert in many,

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:58.690
many things and that's not good.

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:04.520
That's why, in December of
2017, we introduced Kubeflow.

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:08.110
Kubeflow, our goal is to
make it easy for everyone

00:09:08.110 --> 00:09:11.200
to develop, deploy,
and manage portable,

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:13.510
distributed ML on Kubernetes.

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:16.360
What this looks like
is you use Kubernetes

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:17.425
as the unified layer.

00:09:17.425 --> 00:09:20.590
It works on your laptop
via Minikube, Docker EE,

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:23.990
it works on the cloud, it works
on your training environment.

00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:26.920
We have a Kubeflow
deployment that

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:29.020
describes your entire
pipeline and then

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:32.200
you're able to stamp out that
exact same Kubeflow deployment

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:34.420
in multiple locations.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:38.675
Right now, in the box,
we are just at 0.1.

00:09:38.675 --> 00:09:41.050
We have a Jupyter notebook,
we have distributed training,

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:44.560
we have multi-model framework,
we have lots of examples,

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:47.860
and best of all, we use a
tool developed by Heptio

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:50.230
called Ksonnet which
lets you customize

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:52.930
for your own packages,
including private packages that

00:09:52.930 --> 00:09:55.150
are relevant just for yourself.

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:56.770
In the box today
we have this, which

00:09:56.770 --> 00:09:59.470
includes a trainer, a model,
model validation, training

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:02.410
and scale, and then also
serving and monitoring.

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:05.260
With that, I'd like to show you
a quick demo of what it looks

00:10:05.260 --> 00:10:08.270
like to roll out Kubeflow.

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:09.260
So here we are.

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:11.200
We're going to do
a Getting Started

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:12.940
and I'm going to use something.

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:16.090
This is a real live
demo but it types for me

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:17.655
so I can talk while I do it.

00:10:17.655 --> 00:10:19.030
The first thing
we're going to do

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:20.780
is we're actually going
to start in Azure.

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:23.980
I spun up a Kubernetes cluster
last night, it's ready to go,

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:26.230
we switch contacts to
Azure, and now we're

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:29.007
going to introduce
our new Kubeflow app.

00:10:29.007 --> 00:10:29.590
You see there?

00:10:29.590 --> 00:10:33.080
I just type ks--
that's ksonnet--

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:35.500
[? init ?] and it
created the directory.

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:38.390
I switch into the
directory and now I'm

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:40.040
switching over to the context.

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:42.350
I create my brand new
environment locally

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:44.360
and I say, hey, just
letting you know,

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:46.980
we're going to deploy to Azure.

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:49.029
We set the parameter,
telling Kubeflow

00:10:49.029 --> 00:10:51.570
that you're deploying to that
that environment I just set up,

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:54.320
and now we're going to select
the things that matter, right?

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:55.440
The first thing
I'm going to do is

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:56.770
I'm going to add that registry.

00:10:56.770 --> 00:11:00.410
There's just a Kubeflow
