00:25:21.030 --> 00:25:27.030
inside Google and we're going
to simply look at the jsonnet

00:25:27.030 --> 00:25:31.190
and show you that we
defined the data as being

00:25:31.190 --> 00:25:36.050
sourced from the new
Elastifile provision volumes.

00:25:36.050 --> 00:25:39.470
We are also adding a new
volume and that volume

00:25:39.470 --> 00:25:43.220
is essentially where we'll
take the resulting model.

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:45.320
It's called here train out.

00:25:45.320 --> 00:25:50.360
The train out model will also
be placed on an Elastifile data

00:25:50.360 --> 00:25:54.350
containers because as
you will see shortly

00:25:54.350 --> 00:25:59.450
we want to then use that data
in different environments.

00:25:59.450 --> 00:26:03.920
We have everything set
up and we're simply

00:26:03.920 --> 00:26:07.310
going to run the model.

00:26:07.310 --> 00:26:09.740
But before doing that
we'll just show you

00:26:09.740 --> 00:26:13.650
that there is nothing
inside the TF out right now

00:26:13.650 --> 00:26:16.820
because we haven't run
it just yet, but we will.

00:26:20.610 --> 00:26:23.520
And running it is
a simple command

00:26:23.520 --> 00:26:30.150
of executing in
ksonnet applying the TF

00:26:30.150 --> 00:26:33.360
job that we've just created.

00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:39.900
We write apply and we apply
it and a job is created.

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:44.220
We can grab that job and see
it's up here for the 11 seconds

00:26:44.220 --> 00:26:46.090
that we just ran it.

00:26:46.090 --> 00:26:49.710
We can see that the pod is
being created in our Kubernetes

00:26:49.710 --> 00:26:50.980
cluster.

00:26:50.980 --> 00:26:54.190
And this is a small demo
so we're using one part,

00:26:54.190 --> 00:26:58.950
but essentially, as Kubernetes
can grow the number of pods,

00:26:58.950 --> 00:27:02.910
Elastifile can grow in the
back end, the storage side,

00:27:02.910 --> 00:27:07.380
and handle as much
bandwidth with as much IOPS

00:27:07.380 --> 00:27:11.260
as you would need
in the environment.

00:27:11.260 --> 00:27:14.910
We'll take a look and
see that this is running.

00:27:14.910 --> 00:27:18.660
You can either look at the
logs or use the beautiful GUIs

00:27:18.660 --> 00:27:21.450
provided through Kubeflow.

00:27:21.450 --> 00:27:23.280
It is indeed running.

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:28.110
You can monitor either
through API or through the GUI

00:27:28.110 --> 00:27:31.440
the data being used
over Elastifile.

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:35.070
In this case, as we said it's
a very small demo, but again,

00:27:35.070 --> 00:27:37.260
the Elastifile
environment can grow

00:27:37.260 --> 00:27:41.610
to millions of IOPS and
tens of gigabytes per second

00:27:41.610 --> 00:27:44.160
because you do want to
complete this training as

00:27:44.160 --> 00:27:46.140
fast as possible.

00:27:46.140 --> 00:27:48.300
All of this training
is happening

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:53.850
on the very latest data
that appeared in the cloud.

00:27:53.850 --> 00:27:57.030
We just completed
updating the model.

00:27:57.030 --> 00:28:00.000
Again, it's very nice
that we have a new model

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:03.870
but we want to take that
model to where it's useful.

00:28:03.870 --> 00:28:06.710
So we're going to do
just as we did before,

00:28:06.710 --> 00:28:11.640
a replication, this time
of the TF out volume.

00:28:11.640 --> 00:28:14.730
Taking the results
and replicating it

00:28:14.730 --> 00:28:16.960
into a third environment.

00:28:16.960 --> 00:28:19.110
This can be another
region in Google,

00:28:19.110 --> 00:28:23.130
this can be back to on-prem,
this can be anywhere

00:28:23.130 --> 00:28:24.310
with two clicks.

00:28:24.310 --> 00:28:27.990
We are replicating our
model and this makes sure,

00:28:27.990 --> 00:28:31.170
because it's a synchronous
replication that's constantly

00:28:31.170 --> 00:28:37.380
happening, that the model users
have the very latest model.

00:28:37.380 --> 00:28:42.490
And the model, as you recall,
has the very latest data.

00:28:42.490 --> 00:28:43.630
NFS again.

00:28:43.630 --> 00:28:45.820
So no matter what
your application

00:28:45.820 --> 00:28:48.400
is that is going
to run this model,

00:28:48.400 --> 00:28:51.190
you can simply make
a new directory.

00:28:51.190 --> 00:28:54.280
This time the directory
will be from GCP

00:28:54.280 --> 00:28:57.790
because we transferred
that data from GCP

00:28:57.790 --> 00:29:03.670
and we can mount our NFS
volume into that directory.

00:29:03.670 --> 00:29:07.390
Once we mounted this
volume that was replicated,

00:29:07.390 --> 00:29:12.440
we are assured that the data
is available for our processes

00:29:12.440 --> 00:29:15.190
wherever we're
going to run them.

00:29:15.190 --> 00:29:18.760
This can be an IoT environment.

00:29:18.760 --> 00:29:20.980
And this can be
multiple environments

00:29:20.980 --> 00:29:23.260
where we send that model to.

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:27.850
And there is our model and data.

00:29:31.440 --> 00:29:35.550
Our florist just saw this
horrible malignant flower

00:29:35.550 --> 00:29:38.640
in one of his
patients and is going

00:29:38.640 --> 00:29:43.350
to check whether that
is indeed a rose.

00:29:43.350 --> 00:29:46.500
In this case, we're running
a simple Python script that

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:51.600
uses the model to do a
prediction and with 99%

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:54.450
we're assured that
this is a rose

00:29:54.450 --> 00:29:56.850
because the model
that we're using

00:29:56.850 --> 00:30:01.110
was retrained on the very
latest data in the field.

00:30:01.110 --> 00:30:03.300
So there you have it.

00:30:03.300 --> 00:30:06.240
A storage platform
that enables you

00:30:06.240 --> 00:30:10.740
to complement all of the
cross-Cloud capabilities

00:30:10.740 --> 00:30:13.920
and cross-environment
capabilities of Kubeflow

00:30:13.920 --> 00:30:15.660
and TensorFlow.

00:30:15.660 --> 00:30:17.190
Back to you, David.

00:30:17.190 --> 00:30:21.430
DAVID ARONCHICK: So as you can
see, Kubeflow truly is portable

00:30:21.430 --> 00:30:24.270
and it supports all of the
portability of something

00:30:24.270 --> 00:30:27.900
just like Elastifile to help
you move your data as well.

00:30:27.900 --> 00:30:29.280
This is GCB next.

00:30:29.280 --> 00:30:33.020
If we were just doing Kubeflow
0.1, that's not interesting.

00:30:33.020 --> 00:30:34.500
Let's do something cooler.

00:30:34.500 --> 00:30:38.210
Let's introduce Kubeflow 0.2.

00:30:38.210 --> 00:30:42.240
Kubeflow 0.2 takes all the
work that we've done up to date

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:45.300
and extends it even further.

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:47.730
Some of these are new
and improved components

00:30:47.730 --> 00:30:49.230
with some really
advanced stuff I'll

00:30:49.230 --> 00:30:52.720
be showing in just second,
platform integrations,

00:30:52.720 --> 00:30:55.170
specifically taking
advantage of different clouds

00:30:55.170 --> 00:30:58.080
that we deploy to, and a
much simplified getting

00:30:58.080 --> 00:31:00.780
started experience.

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:02.726
Advanced features.

00:31:02.726 --> 00:31:04.350
One of the things
that we really wanted

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:07.410
to do when it came to Kubeflow
was make it a platform.

00:31:07.410 --> 00:31:09.900
We didn't just want
to be providing

00:31:09.900 --> 00:31:11.040
all of these solutions.

00:31:11.040 --> 00:31:14.370
We wanted to let you pick and
choose platform components that

00:31:14.370 --> 00:31:16.500
came from outside, that
came from other companies,

00:31:16.500 --> 00:31:18.390
in this case H2O.

00:31:18.390 --> 00:31:20.400
They had a great demo yesterday.

00:31:20.400 --> 00:31:24.180
If you weren't there,
you can watch it online,

00:31:24.180 --> 00:31:26.070
but basically they're
using Kubeflow

00:31:26.070 --> 00:31:27.955
to take care of a lot
of the core components

00:31:27.955 --> 00:31:29.580
and then they're
differentiating and it

00:31:29.580 --> 00:31:30.930
looks like this basically.

00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:33.810
Where you have a CPU
that's provided underneath,

00:31:33.810 --> 00:31:37.650
you have Kubernetes and Kubeflow
as a layer on top of that,

00:31:37.650 --> 00:31:39.660
and then you're able to
distribute the unique

00:31:39.660 --> 00:31:43.200
components of H2O-- both the
closed source and the open

00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:44.370
source version--

00:31:44.370 --> 00:31:45.540
to run on top of it.

00:31:45.540 --> 00:31:48.540
And then people can build and
engage at that higher level.

00:31:48.540 --> 00:31:51.270
They never even have to think
about what they're running on.

00:31:51.270 --> 00:31:54.450
That's part of the essence of
what we provide with Kubeflow.

00:31:54.450 --> 00:31:57.560
Second, we have a
hyperparamater tuner.

00:31:57.560 --> 00:31:59.310
For those that don't
know, one of the most

00:31:59.310 --> 00:32:01.980
complicating and annoying
things about building a model

00:32:01.980 --> 00:32:03.990
is really tuning
your hyperparameters.

00:32:03.990 --> 00:32:06.300
How big your batch is, how
many epochs you should run,

00:32:06.300 --> 00:32:08.970
what your targets should
be, and things like that.

00:32:08.970 --> 00:32:11.310
In Kubeflow right
now, we have a brand

00:32:11.310 --> 00:32:13.080
new alpha component
called katib,

00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:16.080
which is based on the same
work that we do at Google

00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:17.380
around hyperparameter tuning.

00:32:17.380 --> 00:32:20.370
It's an open source version
and it's all checked in.

00:32:20.370 --> 00:32:21.960
You can see here,
this is just a GIF

00:32:21.960 --> 00:32:24.330
of running through how
you might experiment in

00:32:24.330 --> 00:32:27.750
a whole variety of ways
directly inside Kubeflow

00:32:27.750 --> 00:32:29.970
to explore the most
efficient hyperparameters

00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:32.380
for your system.

00:32:32.380 --> 00:32:36.000
And then finally, we want
to talk about PyTorch.

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:38.190
A lot of people can
make the assumption

00:32:38.190 --> 00:32:40.730
that Kubeflow means
TensorFlow only.

00:32:40.730 --> 00:32:43.740
That flow really means
machine learning flow

00:32:43.740 --> 00:32:47.250
and we really are
deeply committed

00:32:47.250 --> 00:32:49.440
to many, many
different frameworks.

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:51.330
We want to make it
very explicit here

00:32:51.330 --> 00:32:53.910
PyTorch is absolutely supported.

00:32:53.910 --> 00:32:57.810
It's in alpha right now and we
expect to hit beta very soon.

00:32:57.810 --> 00:32:59.910
And to swap it out
here, were those steps

00:32:59.910 --> 00:33:02.160
that you saw earlier, to
swap it out you literally

00:33:02.160 --> 00:33:04.740
say I'm going to cross that
out and I'm going to swap it.

00:33:04.740 --> 00:33:08.740
Presto, you're
now using PyTorch.

00:33:08.740 --> 00:33:11.500
Second, I want to talk
about Platform Integrations.

00:33:11.500 --> 00:33:14.200
One of the things you
saw with Elastifile

00:33:14.200 --> 00:33:16.630
is that Elastifile just did
the work under the hood.

00:33:16.630 --> 00:33:18.820
It just surfaced a
persistent volume

00:33:18.820 --> 00:33:22.000
claim to Kubernetes and
Kubeflow knew how to use that.

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:24.580
That's something that's very
fundamental to Kubeflow.

00:33:24.580 --> 00:33:26.920
We don't want to do the work.

00:33:26.920 --> 00:33:28.960
Think about your various clouds.

00:33:28.960 --> 00:33:31.900
This cloud has A and this
cloud has B, that's great.

00:33:31.900 --> 00:33:35.170
As long as you surface those
elements to Kubernetes,

00:33:35.170 --> 00:33:36.350
we're good.

00:33:36.350 --> 00:33:38.300
And for that, it looks
something like this.

00:33:38.300 --> 00:33:39.850
We want to support
the things that

00:33:39.850 --> 00:33:43.330
are specific to your cloud
around security, data

00:33:43.330 --> 00:33:47.410
exfiltration prevention,
Identity Aware Proxy.

00:33:47.410 --> 00:33:50.410
Second, we want to support
a unique Cloud-specific

00:33:50.410 --> 00:33:51.280
Infrastructure.

00:33:51.280 --> 00:33:56.800
Maybe FPGA, GPU, TPU, things
like pre-emptible VMs.

00:33:56.800 --> 00:33:59.260
And then finally
Kubernetes extensions,

00:33:59.260 --> 00:34:00.970
where people don't
fork Kubernetes

00:34:00.970 --> 00:34:06.460
but they take advantage of the
extensibility of Kubernetes

00:34:06.460 --> 00:34:10.000
to provide unique solutions
for their customers.

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:12.850
These would be things like
node pools and auto scaling

00:34:12.850 --> 00:34:14.540
clusters.

00:34:14.540 --> 00:34:16.900
Now to be clear,
Kubeflow already

00:34:16.900 --> 00:34:19.210
supports all of these today.

00:34:19.210 --> 00:34:21.639
We do it right now
on GKE but there's

00:34:21.639 --> 00:34:23.980
nothing specific about
Kubeflow and GKE.

00:34:23.980 --> 00:34:26.980
It just happens that the GKE
team saw this opportunity

00:34:26.980 --> 00:34:29.679
and built a number of solutions
and connectivity for us.

00:34:29.679 --> 00:34:31.960
This is absolutely
supported anywhere

00:34:31.960 --> 00:34:34.507
that Kubernetes is compliant.

00:34:34.507 --> 00:34:36.340
Specifically, I want
to call out two things.

00:34:36.340 --> 00:34:38.969
First, TPU Acceleration.

00:34:38.969 --> 00:34:42.820
TPU is Google's Tensor
Processing Unit.

00:34:42.820 --> 00:34:45.909
It's designed to make processing
machine workloads very, very

00:34:45.909 --> 00:34:47.530
fast.

00:34:47.530 --> 00:34:50.560
In this case, an
example is around

00:34:50.560 --> 00:34:53.110
using a number of
image processing.

00:34:53.110 --> 00:34:55.690
Before, on a number
of TPUs, you might

00:34:55.690 --> 00:34:59.890
get through about 900,000
images per second and with TPUs

00:34:59.890 --> 00:35:03.890
it's almost twice as
fast at 1.7 million.

00:35:03.890 --> 00:35:06.185
Similarly, cost
to reach accuracy

00:35:06.185 --> 00:35:08.560
when you're doing machine
learning workloads, your number

00:35:08.560 --> 00:35:10.650
one goal is to reach accuracy.

00:35:10.650 --> 00:35:13.450
In this case, to
reach 75% accuracy,

00:35:13.450 --> 00:35:15.650
total dollars when you're
running on non-TPUs

00:35:15.650 --> 00:35:17.380
is about $129.

00:35:17.380 --> 00:35:20.920
On TPUs it's less than
half as much. $55.

00:35:20.920 --> 00:35:25.600
So both faster
and a lower price.

00:35:25.600 --> 00:35:28.180
Second, auto scaling clusters.

00:35:28.180 --> 00:35:31.030
This is probably
the number one thing

00:35:31.030 --> 00:35:34.000
that we hear when it comes to
machine learning workloads.

00:35:34.000 --> 00:35:36.550
Data scientists know
data, they understand

00:35:36.550 --> 00:35:38.350
what their needs
are, but they don't

00:35:38.350 --> 00:35:40.150
want to think about
infrastructure at all.

00:35:40.150 --> 00:35:42.860
And so an example here
is, My model works great,

00:35:42.860 --> 00:35:44.200
the data scientist says.

00:35:44.200 --> 00:35:45.710
I want six nodes.

00:35:45.710 --> 00:35:48.400
So they ask their IT
Ops person to do it

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:51.470
but their IT Ops person
has a lot of things to do

00:35:51.470 --> 00:35:52.840
and that's not great.

00:35:52.840 --> 00:35:55.480
She's very patient.

00:35:55.480 --> 00:35:59.650
But finally the IT Ops
person distributes it,

00:35:59.650 --> 00:36:02.890
creates those six nodes, and
passes it back to the data

00:36:02.890 --> 00:36:04.140
scientist to execute.

00:36:04.140 --> 00:36:06.970
Data scientist does her
work and then she's done.

00:36:06.970 --> 00:36:07.630
She walks away.

00:36:07.630 --> 00:36:11.290
She says, Great, I'm
on to my next problem.

00:36:11.290 --> 00:36:13.400
Unfortunately, they
forgot to turn it off

00:36:13.400 --> 00:36:15.961
and that's lots of money
and that's not great.

00:36:15.961 --> 00:36:18.460
Again, this is something where
you have two different people

00:36:18.460 --> 00:36:20.470
working in two different ways.

00:36:20.470 --> 00:36:22.780
What you really want is
to let the data scientist

00:36:22.780 --> 00:36:24.490
describe what happens
and let Kubernetes

00:36:24.490 --> 00:36:25.970
take care of the rest.

00:36:25.970 --> 00:36:28.450
So that's where we get to auto
scaling TensorFlow for jobs.

00:36:28.450 --> 00:36:32.000
The data scientist
describes what they need.

00:36:32.000 --> 00:36:35.140
CPU, RAM accelerators,
and so on.

00:36:35.140 --> 00:36:37.510
The TPU jobs then
provision themselves--

00:36:37.510 --> 00:36:41.080
and by the way they delete
themselves when they're done--

00:36:41.080 --> 00:36:43.150
and scale back down.

00:36:43.150 --> 00:36:47.330
Please do remember to save
your logs externally to that.

00:36:47.330 --> 00:36:50.400
And then finally they cap based
on maximum scale parameters.

00:36:50.400 --> 00:36:53.610
Your data scientists can't go
and say, I want 10,000 nodes.

00:36:53.610 --> 00:36:55.300
What that looks like is this.

00:36:55.300 --> 00:36:57.580
The data scientist goes
and they say, OK, I'm

00:36:57.580 --> 00:36:59.230
ready to roll out my job.

00:36:59.230 --> 00:37:00.040
Here it is.

00:37:00.040 --> 00:37:02.094
This is an abbreviated version.

00:37:02.094 --> 00:37:04.510
I'll show you what a real one
looks like in just a second.

00:37:04.510 --> 00:37:05.980
They say, I want six.

00:37:05.980 --> 00:37:10.570
I want one CPU, I want one
GPU for each of the nodes,

00:37:10.570 --> 00:37:12.260
and here's the
container to go and run.

00:37:12.260 --> 00:37:16.310
Kubernetes auto-provisions that,
including attaching the GPUs.

00:37:16.310 --> 00:37:20.260
Job is done, nodes
delete themselves,

00:37:20.260 --> 00:37:23.290
and the IT Ops person finds
out cool stuff about YouTube

00:37:23.290 --> 00:37:24.145
during that process.

00:37:24.145 --> 00:37:26.020
Actually, IT Ops person
are going and working

00:37:26.020 --> 00:37:27.250
on really hard things.

00:37:27.250 --> 00:37:28.920
They're just not
dealing with things

00:37:28.920 --> 00:37:30.920
that the data scientist
take care of themselves.

00:37:30.920 --> 00:37:34.405
So with that, let me demo
it for you really quickly.

00:37:34.405 --> 00:37:34.905
OK.

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:39.429
We will pop over here.

00:37:39.429 --> 00:37:39.970
Here you are.

00:37:39.970 --> 00:37:42.780
This is the YAML that you
might use for an auto scaling

00:37:42.780 --> 00:37:43.420
cluster.

00:37:43.420 --> 00:37:45.280
It looks like this.

00:37:45.280 --> 00:37:47.995
Like I said, this is
literally all that's required.

00:37:50.920 --> 00:37:57.100
You can see here I want six
replicas, I want CPU and a GPU,

00:37:57.100 --> 00:37:59.500
and we will get nodes.

00:37:59.500 --> 00:38:02.470
You can see here I
just have three nodes.

00:38:02.470 --> 00:38:08.140
Control create -F and I'm
going to create my training.

00:38:08.140 --> 00:38:09.010
That's it.

00:38:09.010 --> 00:38:11.650
The data scientist has
now submitted her job

00:38:11.650 --> 00:38:14.322
to the cluster and the clusters
are going to go and create it.

00:38:14.322 --> 00:38:16.030
Now I'm going to show
you this and you're

00:38:16.030 --> 00:38:18.190
going to say, wait,
where are the nodes?

00:38:18.190 --> 00:38:21.160
Well the problem is is that
it takes about a minute.

00:38:21.160 --> 00:38:26.717
You don't want wide ranging
nodes spin-up and spin-down.

00:38:26.717 --> 00:38:28.300
So what we'll do is
we'll get the pods

00:38:28.300 --> 00:38:30.340
and I will show you
that you can see

00:38:30.340 --> 00:38:33.760
here all six are
created and are pending

00:38:33.760 --> 00:38:35.425
and we will describe the pod.

00:38:38.420 --> 00:38:41.510
You will see here at the
bottom it says insufficient.

00:38:41.510 --> 00:38:44.330
So what I'd like to
do is let's swap back

00:38:44.330 --> 00:38:46.670
and we'll come back and
check in on this in a second

00:38:46.670 --> 00:38:48.420
because we'll do another
demo in a second.

00:38:50.440 --> 00:38:53.200
Then finally, we want
to do Simplified Setup.

00:38:56.020 --> 00:38:59.170
I know that's just six total
commands for setting up

00:38:59.170 --> 00:39:02.590
Kubeflow but that's
boring and terrible.

00:39:02.590 --> 00:39:04.540
Who would want to
do six commands?

00:39:04.540 --> 00:39:11.140
How about one, say your version,
two, download a script--

00:39:11.140 --> 00:39:14.200
in this case deploy .sh If you
already have it on your system

00:39:14.200 --> 00:39:15.620
you don't even need to redo it.

00:39:15.620 --> 00:39:16.420
Three, you deploy.

00:39:16.420 --> 00:39:17.550
Great.

00:39:17.550 --> 00:39:20.410
Now you're at one command.

00:39:20.410 --> 00:39:23.609
One command is better than
six, even I know that.

00:39:23.609 --> 00:39:25.900
Let's swap over and I'll show
you what that looks like.

00:39:28.470 --> 00:39:30.200
And let's see how
we're doing here.

00:39:32.820 --> 00:39:34.996
We get our nodes and
it's still coming up.

00:39:34.996 --> 00:39:36.495
We'll come back to
that in a second.

00:39:40.014 --> 00:39:40.680
And here we are.

00:39:40.680 --> 00:39:42.180
Easy setup ls.

00:39:46.990 --> 00:39:51.010
I left some old stuff here
so we'll just get rid of you.

00:39:51.010 --> 00:39:52.540
All right.

00:39:52.540 --> 00:39:56.170
You can see here deploy.sh.

00:39:56.170 --> 00:39:57.910
This is a lot of
stuff but really

00:39:57.910 --> 00:39:59.430
it's pretty straightforward.

00:39:59.430 --> 00:40:02.460
It asks for your IP address
and things like that

00:40:02.460 --> 00:40:04.210
but this is the important
part that you're

00:40:04.210 --> 00:40:05.980
going to want to care about.

00:40:05.980 --> 00:40:08.850
You can detail right
there in your deploy.sh

00:40:08.850 --> 00:40:11.140
the packages that you want
but everything else is

00:40:11.140 --> 00:40:12.170
taken care of for you.

00:40:12.170 --> 00:40:15.570
And in this case, I
want to deploy it all.

00:40:15.570 --> 00:40:18.680
I've downloaded it
already and it's done.

00:40:18.680 --> 00:40:19.960
That's it.

00:40:19.960 --> 00:40:25.000
Everything that you saw before
is now being setup as we speak.

00:40:25.000 --> 00:40:27.970
That is exactly what
our intention was.

00:40:27.970 --> 00:40:31.960
To make it that easy to
set up Kubeflow over time.

00:40:31.960 --> 00:40:35.284
Let's go back to see what
our node situation is

00:40:35.284 --> 00:40:36.700
and you can see
they're [? out. ?]

00:40:36.700 --> 00:40:38.530
So it has been
provisioned and it's just

00:40:38.530 --> 00:40:41.620
a matter of coming up and
attaching to the Kubernetes

00:40:41.620 --> 00:40:42.910
cluster.

00:40:42.910 --> 00:40:45.920
You can see there we have the
nodes that we had originally

00:40:45.920 --> 00:40:47.590
and then we added
those six that were

00:40:47.590 --> 00:40:50.470
specific to the
particular workload.

00:40:50.470 --> 00:40:51.430
That's Kubeflow.

00:40:51.430 --> 00:40:54.460
That is what we're
working on to make machine

00:40:54.460 --> 00:40:56.620
learning on Kubernetes easy.

00:40:56.620 --> 00:40:58.270
You didn't see
bespoke solutions,

00:40:58.270 --> 00:41:00.890
you didn't see us having to
extend Kubernetes or fork it

00:41:00.890 --> 00:41:02.620
in some specific way.

00:41:02.620 --> 00:41:04.540
You also didn't see
cloud-specific tech.

00:41:04.540 --> 00:41:06.310
Everything you saw
there is surfaced

00:41:06.310 --> 00:41:08.950
to Kubernetes through standard
extension points and that

00:41:08.950 --> 00:41:11.049
really is key.

00:41:11.049 --> 00:41:13.090
And you didn't see forking
of the Kubernetes API.

00:41:13.090 --> 00:41:17.200
The Kubernetes CRDs are designed
for this kind of extension

00:41:17.200 --> 00:41:20.700
and are absolutely
critical for what we use.

00:41:20.700 --> 00:41:23.790
We've had great momentum
since we got started.

00:41:23.790 --> 00:41:27.680
Over 1,000 commits, 100
community contributors,

00:41:27.680 --> 00:41:30.060
20 plus companies
contributing including

00:41:30.060 --> 00:41:31.930
very, very significant folks.

00:41:31.930 --> 00:41:37.190
[INAUDIBLE],, Alibaba,
Cisco, obviously Google,

00:41:37.190 --> 00:41:39.710
Uber contributed
MPI and Horovod,

00:41:39.710 --> 00:41:42.390
we have Pachyderm for
doing data processing,

00:41:42.390 --> 00:41:45.520
and you can see many of
the other folks there.

00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:48.170
And the reality is is that this
is what we're trying to do.

00:41:48.170 --> 00:41:51.170
As you saw, our goal
is to get to a place

00:41:51.170 --> 00:41:54.720
where data scientists can
operate at that next layer up.

00:41:54.720 --> 00:41:57.140
They shouldn't have
to understand anything

00:41:57.140 --> 00:41:58.880
about their underlying
infrastructure.

00:41:58.880 --> 00:42:00.740
They should focus on
the work that they're

00:42:00.740 --> 00:42:03.140
trying to do because
they're experts at that.

00:42:03.140 --> 00:42:05.150
They're not experts
in infrastructure.

00:42:05.150 --> 00:42:08.870
And it is absolutely our
goal that within five years,

00:42:08.870 --> 00:42:12.260
every paper in the world, every
research, every experiment

00:42:12.260 --> 00:42:17.120
in the world, comes with a
Kubeflow deployment, a pipeline

00:42:17.120 --> 00:42:20.720
that describes how to run
that model and deploy from all

00:42:20.720 --> 00:42:24.110
the way from data all the way up
to production, and sample data.

00:42:24.110 --> 00:42:27.170
And with those three we think we
can make dramatic improvements

00:42:27.170 --> 00:42:29.690
to the overall
reproducibility problem.

00:42:29.690 --> 00:42:31.705
And really, our goal
is to empower these.

00:42:31.705 --> 00:42:33.284
The people not here.

00:42:33.284 --> 00:42:35.450
The people who don't know
anything about Kubernetes.

00:42:35.450 --> 00:42:38.930
We want to give them tools
to make their own decisions

00:42:38.930 --> 00:42:41.540
with their own data.

00:42:41.540 --> 00:42:43.190
And we are just getting started.

00:42:43.190 --> 00:42:49.190
Kubeflow 0.3 is due in weeks,
where weeks is about 12.

00:42:49.190 --> 00:42:53.150
Our roadmap, we're
really marching

00:42:53.150 --> 00:42:55.730
through getting
towards 1.0 and we

00:42:55.730 --> 00:42:57.680
want to bring a lot of
those alpha features

00:42:57.680 --> 00:43:00.170
that you saw forward into beta.

00:43:00.170 --> 00:43:03.219
But also we want to keep
pushing higher level.

00:43:03.219 --> 00:43:04.760
That hyperparameter
tuning right now,

00:43:04.760 --> 00:43:06.170
it requires a
little bit of code.

00:43:06.170 --> 00:43:07.820
Our hope is that we
can accomplish that

00:43:07.820 --> 00:43:09.890
without any code whatsoever.

00:43:09.890 --> 00:43:13.366
We want to provide job operators
right now to support PyTorch.

00:43:13.366 --> 00:43:14.990
It was a little bit
of work but we want

00:43:14.990 --> 00:43:16.281
to support all the frameworks--

00:43:16.281 --> 00:43:19.610
XGBoost and Caffe and MXNet
and all those various things.

00:43:19.610 --> 00:43:21.980
And we're going to do that
through a standard extensible

00:43:21.980 --> 00:43:23.150
framework.

00:43:23.150 --> 00:43:26.270
And then finally we want to get
to things like Click to Deploy.

00:43:26.270 --> 00:43:29.580
Again, data scientists
shouldn't even have to see code.

00:43:29.580 --> 00:43:31.580
They shouldn't even have
to use a deploy script.

00:43:31.580 --> 00:43:33.770
They should be able to do
that behind the scenes.

00:43:33.770 --> 00:43:37.400
Or, better yet, tell us,
because we really are listening.

00:43:37.400 --> 00:43:39.050
It is a wide open community.

00:43:39.050 --> 00:43:42.260
We want to respond to
wherever you're taking it

00:43:42.260 --> 00:43:46.120
and the more scenarios that you
come up with the better it is.

00:43:46.120 --> 00:43:48.680
It will help guide the project.

00:43:48.680 --> 00:43:51.740
And with that, there's
lots of places to connect.

00:43:51.740 --> 00:43:54.800
Please reach out to me,
reach out to Elastifile,

00:43:54.800 --> 00:43:57.350
reach out wherever you'd
like because we really

00:43:57.350 --> 00:43:59.030
are ready to hear
about how you'd

00:43:59.030 --> 00:44:01.670
like to use this and
bring it to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:44:01.670 --> 00:44:05.320
[MUSIC PLAYING]

